UID:7cb86a2278b44d73780514b60a6d@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Academic\,Washington\, DC\,United States
DESCRIPTION:Accounts of meditative attainments such as states of jhāna (a
 bsorption)\, nirodha (cessation of consciousness)\, or luminous pure aware
 ness are familiar from countless stories in Buddhist scriptures. If you ha
 d asked scholars a few decades ago\, they would have suggested folklore st
 udies as the appropriate discipline. Today\, as accounts of meditation pra
 ctice have become widely known\, these states are studied in neuroscientis
 ts’ labs. Just over a year ago\, Professor Eyal Aviv helped to organize 
 a conference that brought together neuroscientists and Buddhist Studies sc
 holars. Instead of the expected convergence of interests\, what emerged wa
 s a series of surprising disagreements. In this talk\, Pr\nCategory: Acade
 mic\nOriginal listing: https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/religion-in-asia-lab
 -grown-bliss-the-prospects-and-challenges-of-the-scientific-study-of-asian
 -meditations
LOCATION:1957 E Street NW\, 1957 E St NW\, Washington DC 20052
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/religion-in-asia-lab-grown-bliss-the-pr
 ospects-and-challenges-of-the-scientific-study-of-asian-meditations
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:gwu-arts
END:VEVENT
